    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you dig a bit deeper into the specific mechanism of how the heat soak affects the friction coefficient in the semi-metallic pads? Like, is it primarily due to surface glazing or is it more about the thermal expansion of the pad material itself causing inconsistencies? I'm trying to understand why the performance \"acts up\" so much during those peak temperature intervals.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The decline in friction coefficient in semi-metallic pads under heavy heat soak is often a combination of factors, but surface glazing is a major contributor. As temperatures rise rapidly, the pad material can undergo phase changes or oxidize on the surface, forming a glassy, low-friction layer known as glaze. Additionally, the thermal expansion of the binder materials within the pad microstructure can introduce physical inconsistencies, leading to inconsistent pad-rotor contact and thus reduced coefficient of friction. This combined effect causes the noticeable performance degradation during peak temperature intervals.",
      "source": "target",
