    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, that's helpful. So if we're seeing this degradation due to glazing and binder expansion, how much of a factor is the cooling rate of the rotor itself once the braking event ends? If the heat stays trapped in the rotor too long, does that create a feedback loop for the next braking cycle where the pads are basically fighting against a pre-heated component?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the rotor's cooling rate significantly contributes to a negative feedback loop in subsequent braking cycles. If the rotors do not effectively shed their absorbed thermal energy, they remain elevated to high temperatures when the next braking event begins. This pre-heating means the pad-rotor interface starts at a higher baseline temperature, immediately accelerating the onset of high-temperature phenomena like glaze formation or thermal softening within the pad material itself. Effectively, a hotter rotor forces the pad into its high-heat soak regime sooner in the next application.",
      "source": "target",
